      "title": "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6969936f-0962-5c85-a387-068dfadc66e5",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{\\prime}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =3 t\\,\\mathbf{i}- 3 t\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3 t - 2)\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =t^{3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+t\\,\\mathbf{j}+(t^{2} + t)\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{\\prime}(t)=\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t)=3 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t)=-3 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t - 2)=3 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t) & =3\\,\\mathbf{i}-3\\,\\mathbf{j}+3\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})=3 t^{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)=1 \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2} + t)=2 t + 1 \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t) & =3 t^{2}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\,\\mathbf{j}+(2 t + 1)\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(3)(t^{3})+(-3)(t)+(3)(t^{2} + t) \\\\  & =3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} \\\\ P_2 & =(3 t)(3 t^{2})+(- 3 t)(1)+(3 t - 2)(2 t + 1) \\\\  & =9 t^{3} + 6 t^{2} - 4 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =12 t^{3} + 9 t^{2} - 4 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
