      ":question/id": "f0c1b7af-c457-5284-99b5-2ffa8a57b45f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨- 2 t^{2} - 2,\\,- 2 t^{3} - 1,\\,3 t⟩$, find $(\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{\\prime}(t).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{\\prime}(t)=\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 t^{2} - 2)=- 4 t \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 t^{3} - 1)=- 6 t^{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t)=3 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨- 4 t,\\,- 6 t^{2},\\,3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 4 t)=-4 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 6 t^{2})=- 12 t \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)=0 \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime\\prime}(t) & =⟨-4,\\,- 12 t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(-4)(- 2 t^{2} - 2)+(- 12 t)(- 2 t^{3} - 1)+(0)(3 t) \\\\  & =24 t^{4} + 8 t^{2} + 12 t + 8 \\\\ P_2 & =(- 4 t)(- 4 t)+(- 6 t^{2})(- 6 t^{2})+(3)(3) \\\\  & =36 t^{4} + 16 t^{2} + 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =60 t^{4} + 24 t^{2} + 12 t + 17\\end{aligned}\n$$",
